[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 1, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a simple purple line icon resembling a stylized planet or atom with orbiting dots. It has no scientific figure panels, labels, biological source objects, transformations, model interfaces, plotted data, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 16, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled **a**, **b**, and **c** describing **ProCyon**, a unified latent-space framework for protein and phenotype tasks.\n\nPanel **a** shows a table-like overview of tasks under “ProCyon” and “Unified latent space of proteins and phenotypes.” Visible task rows include:\n- **Protein function retrieval**\n- **Domain based protein retrieval**\n- **Protein interaction prediction**\n- **Disease associated protein retrieval**\n- **Contextual target retrieval**\n\nEach row pairs a task name with example prompts and outputs, such as retrieving catalytic proteins in glycolysis, proteins with SH3 domains, interacting proteins involved in autophagy, proteins involved in Parkinson’s disease pathology, and drug-target domain identification.\n\nPanel **b** shows two workflow diagrams:\n- **Protein Retrieval Prompt**: a user provides a disease description, which is processed through a **Protein LM**, **LLM**, and retrieval model against a large protein library, producing retrieved proteins with scores.\n- **Phenotype Generation Prompt**: a user provides a protein input, processed through a **Protein LM**, **structural encoder**, **LLM**, and an **autoregressive text decoder**, producing phenotype descriptions such as disease associations.\n\nPanel **c** shows a radial bar chart summarizing task/data categories by sample count. Categories are grouped around the chart as:\n- **Function**\n- **Disease**\n- **Therapeutics**\n- **Domain**\n- **Interaction**\n\nVisible sources or labels include **GO Function**, **GO Process**, **GO Component**, **Reactome**, **UniProt**, **Enzyme Commission**, **DisGeNET**, **OMIM**, **DrugBank Carrier**, **DrugBank Enzyme**, **DrugBank Target**, **DrugBank Transporter**, **GDA**, **Pfam**, **STRING Experimental**, **STRING Textmining**, and **STRING Coexpression**. The radial scale indicates sample counts from about **1,000** up to **3,000,000**."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 17, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical machine-learning figure labeled **a-h** about **ProCyon** and retrieval of biological functions/components/pathways/diseases/drugs from protein or prompt embeddings.\n\nVisible content:\n\n- **Panel a:** Schematic user/model interface. A user gives a task asking for cellular component and biological process descriptions. Example input mentions the **cytoskeleton** and **nucleus**. The ProCyon output shows ranked protein structure thumbnails with percentile scores around **99.90, 99.74, 97.34**.\n\n- **Panel b:** Bar-chart benchmarks of **retrieval Fmax** across task types:\n  - Protein function\n  - Cellular component\n  - Biological pathway\n  - Disease association\n  - Drug target\n  - Drug-protein interaction  \n  Methods compared include **Random**, **BLAST+KNN**, **ESM2-KNN**, **ESM2-MLP**, **ESM3-KNN**, **ESM3-MLP**, **GearNet-KNN**, **GearNet-MLP**, **BioTranslator**, **ProtST**, and **ProCyon**. ProCyon generally appears among the strongest methods.\n\n- **Panel c:** ProCyon instruction split evaluation with grouped purple bars for **Many-shot**, **Few-shot**, and **Zero-shot** across the same retrieval categories. Metrics are **retrieval Fmax**.\n\n- **Panel d:** Protein source object example: **Q12948: FOXC1**, shown as a protein structure/ribbon graphic. Arrows connect it to phenotype labels:\n  - **Heart development**\n  - **Regulation of HPSC differentiation**\n  - **Regulation of mitotic cell cycle**\n\n- **Panel e:** Bar chart comparing prompt strategies across proteins annotated with **Two functions**, **Three functions**, and **Four functions**. Methods include **ProCyon**, **Precomposite prompt**, **Aggregated individual ranks**, **ProtST**, **Composite prompt**, and **Aggregated individual ranks**.\n\n- **Panel f:** Box/strip plots showing percentile distributions by line of evidence:\n  - **Genetics**\n  - **Somatic mutation**\n  - **Animal model**\n  - **Molecular pathway**\n  - **Literature**\n  - **Drug mechanism of action**\n\n- **Panel g:** Disease retrieval system prompt example for **Autism spectrum disorder**. Text snippets highlight diagnostic/supporting-associated phrases such as persistent impairment in reciprocal social communication, restricted repetitive behavior, intellectual impairment, and language impairment. A percentile plot appears at right.\n\n- **Panel h:** Embedding-space visualization with gray protein embeddings and colored prompt embeddings. Labels include **Basic phenomenon**, **Mechanistic insights**, and **Precise function**. A point labeled **STING** appears at lower right. Bar chart at left shows percentile increases for prompts containing combinations of labels **B**, **M**, and **P**, with significance markers.\n\nOverall finding shown visually: ProCyon appears to support text-prompted biological retrieval across protein function, cellular component, pathway, disease, drug target, and drug-protein interaction tasks, with strong benchmark performance and prompt-composition effects."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 18, "caption": "Figure 3: PROCYON generates accurate responses and phenotype descriptions from multimodal prompts. a) In QA, PROCYON receives a multimodal prompt (protein + phenotype description) and outputs a yes/no answer. b) Benchmarking QA accuracy across models and knowledge domains. Error bars show bootstrapped 95% CIs. c) PROCYON's QA accuracy across different PROCYON-INSTRUCT splits. d) PROCYON tokenizes protein sequences and 3D structures using a multimodal encoder; LLMs must use text surrogates, many of which are not generalizable (gene symbol, UniProt ID). e) QA performance comparison between PROCYON and LLMs using HGNC gene symbols. f) In phenotype generation, PROCYON processes multimodal inputs (proteins, natural language, small molecules) to output open-ended phenotype descriptions. g) Phenotype generation benchmark using reference text similarity (mean BERTScore F 1 ). Hatching indicates ability to generalize to unseen proteins. h) Phenotype generation benchmark using LLM-based judging. Win rate refers to preference over PROCYON; models grouped by parameter count.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled **a-h** describing and evaluating **ProCyon**, a protein-focused question answering/phenotype generation model.\n\nPanel details:\n- **a**: Schematic of a **Question answering** task. A user provides a protein sequence and disease description, shown with a protein structure illustration. Example asks whether a protein is associated with multiple sclerosis. ProCyon outputs a binary answer, shown as **Yes/No** with green check and red cross icons.\n- **b**: Six horizontal bar charts comparing model accuracy across tasks: **Protein function**, **Cellular component**, **Biological pathway**, **Disease association**, **Drug target**, and **Drug-protein interaction**. Models include **BLAST-kNN**, **ESM2-kNN**, **ESM2-MLP**, **ESM3-kNN**, **ESM3-MLP**, **GearNet-kNN**, **GearNet-MLP**, **ProtLLM**, and **ProCyon**. ProCyon is generally among the best, especially for cellular component, biological pathway, disease association, and drug-protein interaction.\n- **c**: Horizontal bar chart comparing **many-shot**, **few-shot**, and **zero-shot** ProCyon-Instruct split performance across the same biological tasks. Accuracy values are plotted for protein function, cellular component, biological pathway, drug target, drug-protein interaction, and disease association.\n- **d**: Workflow diagram comparing ProCyon with conventional LLM interfaces. Input includes a user question, a protein sequence, and a structure image. ProCyon uses **sequence structure tokenization**, pretrained on **60M sequences** and **800K structures**, to answer molecular function questions and generalize to novel proteins. Conventional LLM variants use text identifiers such as **HGNC gene symbol**, **UniProt ID**, or full sequence; check/cross marks indicate relative success/failure.\n- **e**: Three vertical grouped bar charts for **Function**, **Pathway**, and **Disease**, comparing **Llama-HG**, **Mistral-HG**, **GPT4-HG**, and **ProCyon** accuracy. ProCyon performs competitively, with strong pathway accuracy.\n- **f**: Schematic of **Phenotype generation**. User provides a protein sequence/structure and drug indication text; ProCyon generates a mechanistic response. Example involves **acetylsalicylic acid (ASA)** and **COX-1 inhibition**.\n- **g**: Bar charts showing **BERTScore** for out-of-vocabulary generalization across **Function**, **Pathway**, and **Disease**. Models include **ProCyon**, **GPT-AA**, **GPT-AAB**, **GPT-UP**, and **GPT-HG**.\n- **h**: Bar charts showing **win rate vs. ProCyon** for phenotype generation under **Small**, **Medium**, and **Frontier** model groups, comparing Llama-HG, Mistral-HG, GPT4-AA, GPT4-UP, and GPT4-HG across function, pathway, and disease settings.\n\nVisible biological source objects and modalities:\n- Protein amino-acid sequences.\n- Protein structure renderings.\n- Disease descriptions.\n- Drug indication text.\n- Gene/protein identifiers such as HGNC gene symbols and UniProt IDs.\n- Biological annotations including molecular function, cellular component, biological pathway, disease association, drug target, and drug-protein interaction.\n\nMain finding conveyed:\n- ProCyon is presented as a multimodal protein-language model using sequence and structure tokens, outperforming or matching baselines across protein QA and phenotype generation tasks, with highlighted generalization to novel or out-of-vocabulary proteins."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 19, "caption": "Figure 4: PROCYON models domains, peptides, and small molecules beyond proteins. a) PROCYON predicts the small molecule binding domain on proteins, where the input is the description and molecular structure of a drug, and the output is a ranked list of domains on a target protein. In this example, PROCYON retrieves domains of MGAM given the drug miglitol and correctly prioritizes the binding domain for miglitol as the first among the MGAM's nine domains. Each domain is identified by the Pfam ID and the index of the first amino acid in the domain. b) PROCYON ranks the correct drug-binding domain (highlighted in orange) on respective target proteins highly. In Q06187, the green domain ( PF00017 281 ) denotes the PROCYON-predicted binding domain, but the orange domain ( PF07714 402 ) is the correct binding domain. PROCYON predicts the binding domain as top-ranked in 24 examples, second-ranked in 5 examples, and so on. c) PROCYON can be finetuned to predict protein-peptide binding. Left, PROCYON is first finetuned on naturally occurring protein-peptide complexes from the Protein Data Bank (PDB), then tested on an experimental dataset of synthetic peptides screened for binding to the ACE2 protein. Right, the predicted binding scores for binders versus non-binders. Two-sided Mann-Whitney U test. d) Leveraging PROCYON to perform indication-specific drug target retrieval. In this example, the input consists of a novel task definition, the description of indications (nicotine addiction or major depressive disorder), and the name and structure of bupropion. PROCYON ranks known targets (norepinephrine transporter (NET), dopamine transporter (DAT), and nicotinic acetylcholinergic receptor (AChR)) of bupropion among the top 40/18,174 human proteins. Shown are 95% confidence intervals derived by perturbing words in the input prompt. ** p -value < 0 . 001 ; two-sided Wilcoxon signed rank test.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled **a-d** describing **ProCyon**, a model/prompting framework for protein-related prediction tasks.\n\nPanel **a** shows a protein structure labeled **O43451 (MGAM)**, with an **orange binding domain** highlighted. The workflow says the protein is **decomposed into domains**, then an **input prompt** is constructed. The prompt includes a task description asking whether text describes a drug and which domains bind the drug, an input text about **miglitol**, a drug structure, and a list of domains for MGAM. The output shown as **ProCyon** is a ranked domain table with scores; top domains include **PF01055_349** and **PF01055_2111**.\n\nPanel **b** shows three protein examples with structures and drug targets: **P00742 / Apixaban**, **Q13451 / Sirolimus**, and **O05187 / Ibrutinib**. Each has a domain-score table. A bar chart below reports the **number of protein-drug binding examples** by predicted domain rank, with counts **24, 5, 4, 2, 0** for ranks 1-5, indicating many examples have the binding domain ranked first.\n\nPanel **c** shows a **ProCyon-Bind** training/testing setup. Training uses **natural protein-peptide complexes**; testing uses **synthetic ACE2 peptide binders**, including a synthetic peptide generation schematic and an **ACE2 protein** structure. A violin plot compares predicted binding scores for **Peptide ACE2 binder** vs **Peptide ACE2 non-binder**, with a reported **p = 1.04e-4**, suggesting higher scores for binders.\n\nPanel **d** shows text-based biomedical/drug prompt examples for **Smoking cessation** and **Major depressive disorder**. Inputs contain disease descriptions and drug structures, including **Bupropion**. Downstream diagrams link predicted **disease** and **drug information** to **18.7k human proteins**, with a ranked comparison plot for MDD showing methods labeled **NET**, **DAT**, and **AChR**, and significance brackets."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 20, "caption": "Figure 5: PROCYON characterizes poorly-annotated proteins and their functions. a) PROCYON generates phenotype predictions for AKNAD1. QA filtering selects the highest-confidence outputs; two of the three predicted phenotypes are experimentally supported. b) Additional PROCYON-generated phenotypes for other poorly characterized proteins. All proteins either have no UniProt-annotated function or have no UniProt function as of the PROCYON knowledge cutoff date. c) External evaluation of PROCYON using perturbation datasets. Parallel pathway analysis on genetic perturbation data is used to validate PROCYON-predicted functions for uncharacterized proteins. d) Top 100 functions prioritized by PROCYON for each protein. Percentile refers to the percentile of that protein among all 18,174 human proteins in our database for the specific function query. Green dots indicate overlap with perturbation-derived pathways; grey dots indicate other predictions. ** p < 0 . 005 , * p < 0 . 05 ; hypergeometric test. e) PROCYON-generated pathways for poorly characterized proteins associated with Parkinson's disease (PD). Left, heatmap of enrichment scores for each pathway compared to established PD-related proteins ('PD assoc.'), proteins expressed in the nervous system but not in PD or other neurodegenerative conditions ('Neuro control'), and proteins not expressed in brain tissues ('General control'), respectively. Right, differences in enrichment scores between 'PD assoc.' and 'Neuro control' and 'General control'. Statistically significant differences shown in green. Pathways are grouped by PD association score provided by human experts (right).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical AI/workflow figure labeled `a` through `e`.\n\nPanel `a`: Shows a “User” prompt/input box asking to describe the function of a protein from sequence and structure. Example protein is `AKNAD1 (Q5T1N1)`, annotated “No UniProt function.” The input includes a protein sequence snippet and a small protein structure rendering. The query is passed to a `ProCyon` model/interface, followed by “QA Filtering.” Outputs are generated phenotype/function statements with confidence-style marks: two accepted with green check marks and one uncertain with an orange question mark. One accepted statement concerns cytosolic machinery required for formation of the actin cytoskeleton and possible dendrite formation. Another concerns spermatogenesis. Supporting evidence shown includes a subcellular localization image labeled `HPA032070`, with nucleus/microtubules/protein color legend, and single-cell enrichment values for late spermatid and early spermatid. A third generated statement about cAMP-dependent protein kinase activity is marked as having no external evidence.\n\nPanel `b`: Shows examples comparing protein annotations and generated functions. Proteins include `LYRM2 (Q9NU23)`, `ANKDD1A (Q495B1)`, and `UQCC4 (Q4G0I0)`. Each example has a UniProt function status and a ProCyon-generated statement in a purple-bordered box. Blue dashed boxes show UniProt function text added later, implying validation by subsequent database updates. LYRM2 is linked to mitochondrial complex I / N-module integration into mitochondrial respiratory chain complex I. UQCC4 is linked to mitochondrial cytochrome b-c1 complex assembly/stability and respiratory complex III. ANKDD1A has a generated “inhibits cell proliferation” phenotype with a green check mark.\n\nPanel `c`: Schematic of an experimental assay and `ProCyon analysis`. A CRISPR perturbation workflow acts on a protein/cell system, producing pathway analysis and phenotypes. Protein and phenotype inputs feed into ProCyon analysis, which returns retrieved pathways.\n\nPanel `d`: Dot/percentile plot for several protein identifiers, with x-axis labeled `Percentile` from about 80 to 100. Rows include identifiers such as `Q32NC0`, `Q8IXQ4`, `A0A1W2PQ72`, `Q9Y4E6`, `Q49AR2`, `O75391`, `P0DMU8`, `Q5U649`, and `Q86T20`. Green points indicate high percentile ranks; some rows have significance stars.\n\nPanel `e`: Large evaluation plot of generated phenotypes. Left side lists phenotype/pathway terms such as proteasome-mediated ubiquitin-dependent protein catabolic process, lysosomal transport, response to oxidative stress, vesicle-mediated transport, mitochondrial membrane organization, lipid metabolic process, mRNA processing, ATP transport, protein kinase activity, regulation of cell cycle phase transition, cell population proliferation, response to metal ion, regulation of cell cycle, transforming growth factor beta receptor signaling pathway, transcription by RNA polymerase II, extracellular matrix assembly, epigenetic regulation of gene expression, and spermatogenesis. A heatmap shows enrichment scores for conditions labeled near the top, including Parkinson’s disease association versus neuro/general controls. Adjacent horizontal bar charts compare `PD assoc. - Neuro control` and `PD assoc. - General control`, with green bars and gray reference/control bars. A vertical legend at right shows human expert score categories: `0 = Not associated`, `1 = Weak association`, `2 = Moderate association`, `3 = Strong association`.\n\nOverall finding: The figure presents ProCyon as a protein/function and phenotype prediction/retrieval model using sequence/structure inputs, QA filtering, external biological evidence, UniProt update comparisons, CRISPR assay analysis, percentile rankings, and enrichment/human expert scoring for generated phenotypes."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 21, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical figure about multiple sclerosis tissue/RNA analysis and gene/pathway findings.\n\nVisible panels and content:\n- **a**: Workflow schematic showing brain tissue/tissue staining, RNA extraction, RNA sequencing, and computational analysis. Includes icons of brain tissue, microscope slide, RNA/tube, and computer monitors.\n- **b**: Photograph of a dissected coronal human brain section.\n- **c**: Histology image labeled **LFB staining**, showing blue-stained tissue.\n- **d**: Histology/immunostaining image labeled **HLA-immunostaining**, with red highlighted signal over tissue background.\n- **e**: Immunostaining image labeled **MOG immunostaining**, brown myelin-associated staining.\n- **f**: Immunostaining image labeled **HUC/D immunostaining**, with brown cellular staining.\n- **h**: Prompt/interface-style panel with text prompts about multiple sclerosis, cortical gray matter, white matter, active demyelinating lesions, and chronic inflammation. To the right are box/point percentile plots for categories such as **SFG HLA+ in gray matter**, **SFG HLA+ in white matter**, **presence of active demyelinating lesions**, **SFG white matter lesions**, and **meningeal/perivascular inflammation**.\n- **Top right panel**: Volcano/scatter plot with x-axis **Estimate (Regression Coefficient)** and y-axis **-Log10(P-value)**. Significant genes are highlighted in red. Labeled genes include **HLA-DRB5, HLA-DQB1, HLA-DRB1, CXCR4, IGKC, IGHG1, IGHG3, IGHG4, IGLC2, IGLC3, COL1A1, GPNMB, CPM, ZNF727, ADAD2**.\n- **i**: Ranked/percentile gene scatter plot labeled **DEG validation list**, with red points for true **GM-HLA+ DEG** genes. Labeled genes include **TREM2, CXCL16, CD74, MS4A7, HLA-DPB1, HLA-DRB1, HLA-DRB5, HLA-DRA, HLA-DMB, IGHG3, CXCR4, OLIG1, CPM, EVI2B, GPNMB, ADAD2, TFEC, ZNF727, TNFRSF1B, LAIR1, SIGLEC8**.\n- **j**: Gene-level percentile plot for **ZNF727, ADAD2, CPM, EVI2B, MS4A7, SIGLEC8, TFEC, TMEM86B**. Colored dots and x-marks distinguish known annotation status and pathway categories, including CNS inflammatory, inflammatory/immune and MS pathways, inflammatory/immune but no MS pathways, and no inflammatory/immune or MS pathways. Purple vertical marks indicate multiple sclerosis.\n- **k**: Heatmap/table of enriched or predicted biological processes by ProCyon rank for genes including **ZNF727, EVI2B, MS4A7**. Terms visible include protein localization to chromatin, negative regulation of receptor binding, cardiac conduction, regulation of T cell receptor signaling pathway, transcription initiation at RNA polymerase II promoter, positive regulation of Notch signaling pathway, regulation of miRNA transcription, respiratory chain complex IV assembly, stem cell differentiation, neuron projection extension, response to antibiotic, mismatch repair, and acetyl-CoA metabolic process.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Human brain tissue/coronal section.\n- Multiple sclerosis-associated brain regions/lesions.\n- Stained tissue sections and immunostained cells/myelin.\n- RNA-seq-derived gene expression/significance results.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Tissue staining and immunostaining.\n- RNA extraction and RNA sequencing.\n- Regression/significance analysis shown as volcano plot.\n- DEG validation/ranking shown by percentile plots.\n- Prompt-based or model-query interface shown in panel h.\n- ProCyon-predicted biological process ranking shown as heatmap in panel k.\n\nVisible findings:\n- HLA-related immune genes and immunoglobulin genes are among significant positive associations.\n- GM-HLA+ DEG validation list contains many immune/inflammatory genes at high percentiles.\n- Several candidate genes, including **ZNF727, EVI2B, MS4A7, SIGLEC8, TFEC, TMEM86B**, are evaluated by percentile and pathway annotation.\n- Heatmap suggests predicted biological process associations for selected genes, with varying rank/intensity across columns."]}]

